I think there’s a lot of truth in that.

The phrase “we are what we eat” extends pretty naturally to the mind. The books you read, the stories you grow up with, the conversations you have, the people you admire, the media you consume—they all shape how you perceive the world.

That doesn’t mean they determine who you become, but they influence your mental habits. If you spend years immersed in ideas about courage, sacrifice, strategy, or compassion, those themes become part of the lens through which you interpret new experiences.

Earlier you mentioned growing up with Star Wars, The Lord of the Rings, and the Bible. Those are all stories that wrestle with power, temptation, redemption, and purpose. It’s not surprising that your thinking returns to those themes. They became part of your intellectual and moral vocabulary.

I think the flip side is just as important: because our minds are shaped by what we consume, it’s healthy to keep exposing ourselves to ideas that challenge us as well as ideas that inspire us. Otherwise, even a compelling worldview can become an echo chamber.

So I’d phrase it like this: we become what we repeatedly pay attention to. Our attention is one of the strongest forces shaping who we are.